@Peter

Was today the day you thought "I'm mad a heck and just can't take it anymore" or did you really find this even simpler than some other recent Thursday puzzles?

I'm genuinely curious, because while it was pretty straightforward, I found many recent Thursday's even easier than this one.

@Peter
Agreed. This was a personal best time for me.

I feel like I'm screaming into the void here, but I am getting so, so sick of this.

@Francis

Well said.

Sometimes it helps to know there are non BIASED views, no?

@Mary

Thanks for the support, Mary. Very well said. But I wouldn’t expect to say D’s opinion. That’s why I no longer engage directly with D. 

As can be seen in other parts of the paper, some throw out accusations to compensate for their own deficiencies.

@Steve L
And some spend their days using the comments section to feel better than others. Sad.

But hey, if that’s all you have in life…

@David Johnson

A sidekick is an assistant.  "Short" is an abbreviation marker, so ASST.

AMA stands for "Ask Me Anything," which is what Reddit calls their question-and-answer session.  "Sesh" and "Q&A" both signal an abbreviation.

@Mike

And when they found some bugs, it really threw them for loop.

(Good thing there was no Python.)

@Mike 
URL right about all of this. 

🌻☮️

@SP

I made the mistake of starting your last one late in the evening.  Not this time, pal!  I'll try wrangling with it tomorrow.  :)

Thanks for sharing it.

@SP

I made the right move to wait and not try this while sleepy.  Wow that was hard.  Diabolical.  Ikea was the only thing even close to a gimme in the whole durned thing.

What I mean is, thanks!

@SP
Nice. Difficulty level harked back a little, at least for me, and that was welcome. Finished with no help in an hour but did need to run the vowels at the crossing of 122D and 129A. Although now that I look at it, that crossing makes sense. And now I know one more Spanish word. 

Thank you!

@SP

Wow, that's awfully serious and sorry to hear you being on such a downer.  I've noticed you participating more recently but not on a level I'd call amateur moderation.  There are one or two folks here who fit that category and tend to be way, way more off putting than I've ever seen you be.  (It's always refreshing and relieving when they cool their jets for a bit.)

Anyway, don't feel bad.  I don't think I've ever seen you be ungracious and you exhibit far less snark or snidery than others (including me).

@SP

Welcome to the drama queen club!

(Seriously though, keep on posting however you enjoy it - as you know, your comments are among my favorites, even if the puns they often feature are abysmal 🤣)

@SP

Your posts very often say what I'm thinking, and I appreciate that. It's nice to know people like you are around, even if you decide not to post in a "keeper of the peace" capacity anymore.

For the record, you made me feel welcome here when you remembered who I was and mentioned you liked a game I recommended. I was really touched. Thanks for being a great person <3

@SP

Do what you need to do to get where you'd like to be. It is always nice to know there is a kind person who cares.

Keep smiling!

@HeathieJ I had Closet Set at first too, which got me wondering if that is a literal thing? I mean... I assume they're standing up? How do they get the lights in there? Why a closet? Must get hot... 

Then when I thought I'd finished but didn't get my congratulatory jingle, I realized I had a mistake somewhere. Took 3 rounds of scouring before it hit me that 'skit' was wrong... let's change that to a 'd'.

Oh, ok. Gotcha. Closed Set. 🤣

@HeathieJ

Everything was simple and easy to access until one day I decided to read a story on my laptop instead of my iPad. Big mistake! That started the endless loop of verification. You’d think someone would realize that many of us have more than one portal of entry…

But as you say, first world problems, and I get why they’re trying to increase their subscriber base. I just wish they could do it without hassling their existing clientele. What’s the opposite of a hip hip harrumba? Abmurrah pih pih! That’s what I say.

@HeathieJ
I just thought: if it's medically necessary, does that mean you can claim crossword costs under your insurance? Is it tax-deductible? 
(Go ahead and try it. Trump's filed all the IRS reviewers.  They'll never notice. 😏)

@Sara Yes, this kind of thing happens a lot. 

Many, many others have commented on the "gatekeeping" as well.

I've commented on it so often that a few people insisted to me that no one else ever has a problem with it, so I must be making it up! 🙃

@HeathieJ

“I could be wrong but I don't often see anyone criticizing people who are just honestly saying that they don't like these kind of puzzles. Or that it was tough and they struggled and they didn't understand.“

Surely you’re joking

@D
Not sure what the problem is with skipping Thursdays if you don't like them. Wouldn't that be better than asking for there not to be tricks on Thursdays, in view of all the people who do like them? 

Did you just explain that you don't like Thursday tricks or did you complain about them? I do notice that complaints tend to get complaining responses. But if you got a response ruder than your comment, which sometimes happens, I am sorry about that.

@D

I'm not. What I do see and tried to convey is that the tone of a negative reviewer is often met with a like tone.....  For better or worse. 

We obviously have very different perspectives about things, and I've never tried to convince you otherwise.

@HeathieJ I enjoy your posts (I admit I was always smile when you write "har!" at the end), but it happens a lot. When I started on here, there were a lot of comments where people would say they didn't like the puzzle, and the responses would be like, "Maybe a kids' crossword is more your speed," or "That clue was ridiculously easy from the crosses," or "You have to learn and use your brain if you're going to a good solver."

Thankfully, you don't see that as much anymore, but there are some strange comments like, "You can't just say you hate it. You have to tell us why," or "When you say this wasn't your favorite puzzle, you are basically spitying in the constructor's face," or "I'm ashamed you're from Maine. You have issues and you're obviously a teenager!" (Okay, that last one was directed at me.)

We could all be nicer. There are some very rude comments on here. Some of mine included.

@HeathieJ
Honestly you’re one of the nicer commenters here,

But the selective vision is astonishing.

@Red Carpet

The neighboring sections are there.  They're just stacked vertically rather than side-by-side.  56d-1d, etc.

Perhaps a Möbius scroll?

@Al  in Pittsburgh

Möbius scroll is the perfect description of it.  Thank you!!! 

Now I can sleep!!! (I’m not kidding.)

@Ms. Billie M. Spaight

I have been playing the crossword daily for three years now.  I don’t often post nits, and I am aware of the day of the week. 

This was fair constructive criticism.

@Matt

Right?

Also, Obfuscator could be the nickname of your fave setter, David Kwong.

@Andrzej

Your English has failed you here, towarzysz.

I think Gridmancer or Enigmaster are the neologisms you were looking for here.

@Matt

Perhaps, but how would an actual infinite scroll even work in this case? Would the grid just get longer and longer, until endless empty squares become the nightmare of our lives?

I think this was a perfectly acceptable revealer, in line with the concept that clues don’t have to be precise definitions. If it provides enough of a hint to point most people in the right direction, hasn’t it done its job?

@Heidi

Weak gruel.

Revealers are supposed to be clever.

@dutchiris

😂😂😂😂😂😂

Based on what I know of both you and Francis from out here, I thought that very same thing of your age difference, though not with your very comical Dead Sea scrolls reference. 😂😂😂😂😂😂

@Sara. I often feel the same way!  Often though, surprisingly suddenly after a long period of frustration, I see something, and suddenly a bunch of the puzzle is solved.  Happened with today’s puzzle.   

At first I thought there were going to be no down clues at all, and then I found a few and that buoyed me.  Somehow the light lit up on DONOR CARD.  Then the puzzle came together.

I had to correct king/queen bees to beds to complete.

