Ruined the "flow."

@Charlie et al

Paper and pen, folks.
There's even room for doodles and your grocery list.

@Trevor 
Too bad, it's actually pretty good puzzle; and once you understand what the constructor is doing, the gimmick actually makes it easier to solve. 
In fairness, I have seen puzzles where the constructor went way over the line, past clever, past clever-clever to "Hop up and down with one eye closed while twirling your finger around your head and singing the first 20 words of lorem ipsum." But this isn't one of those.

@George @D @Katie
Katie, if you must leave, I will be sorry to see you go. Within this straitened conduit, you seem a fine person (after all, you are Minnesotan) and you are clearly not alone in your sentiments. This site's fee is not trivial. I too have threatened to leave and yet here I am, presumably to the annoyance of George, D, and who knows how many others.
@George @D, I am sorry that you felt the need to speed the departing guest. If you couldn't resist the urge to comment – a problem I can sympathize with 😏 , ask yourself what purpose your post serves, other than adding insult and irritation to the mix? (I had a perfectly ripping opening comment which I've deleted on this principle.) This little piece of bandwidth is probably the only connection we'll ever have. Just skip to the next post if someone (including me) irritates you. I give you permission. 😉

@D,

So now you’re exhibiting the same behavior that you so despise in others?

I don’t get it.

@Laura Stratton

Sample size of 1, for whatever it’s worth: I found today’s puzzle to be quite easy and finished it 27% faster than my recent Thursday average according to xwstats (which looks at Thursdays from the last 60 days).

Puzzle difficulty is highly personal — sometimes I have found a puzzle unusually challenging when the xwstats solve times say that most people found it easy, and vice versa. But my own experience with today’s puzzle aligns with the numbers on xwstats.

@Ian
Upon opening the puzzle, I immediately guessed 1A was either "pals" or MACS, and put in the wrong one first try.

MAC has been used this way in these puzzles a lot, so I've gotten used to it. Was surprised the first time I came across it but then vaguely remember it being used this way in old novels.

@Ian

Oh, it’s not original — if I had to guess, I’d suspect MAC has been clues that way at least 10-20 times in previous NYT puzzles.

And I’ll tell ya, the first couple times I encountered it, I was thrown for quite a loop.

@Ryan
I was stuck at the same spot. Finally broke into it through SEMI COLON, which convinced me to take pAlS out of 1A. 

The trick clicked for me at WHITE BEAN and SWEET SPOT, which finally convinced me to take "earn" and "acai" out of the top right.

@B

Thanks. Another commenter mentioned that. I'll try to show up more often.

(But how can you miss me if I don't go away?)

@John W.

What Sean said ^^^. You would be hard pressed to find any modern constructor who’s not using construction software, and I can guarantee it’s an improvement over anyone not using it. That said, this does not take the place of the constructor’s creativity. You can update your own word lists and add to them. You can play around with it for as long as you want until you are satisfied with the fill and that it reflects your own personality. And of course the theme idea and theme entries are all the constructor’s, for the most part. We’ve seen some tricky themes like anagrams where the entries are generated from a computer program list, but the constructor still has to pick the best entries  (and I’ll admit, sometimes those are noticeable and not always my favorite).

As for AI for the most part it’s just not there yet. I’ve tried occasionally to see if AI can come up with some options for theme fill but almost invariably it just doesn’t get it and my own are better. Sometimes it points me in a positive direction but we are pretty far from AIs creating very creative, high level crosswords like we see in the NYT.

@Barry Ancona

Hilarious!

No disrespect to John W.

@Eric
But the constructors are not asking the software to build the narrative! They are just using it to compare different sets of interlocking words to choose the most interesting from which they, the constructors, can build an interesting grid around.

In short, they are using it consistent with the guardrails for use of generative AI in general (which they are NOT using). They are using algorithms to do something algorithms can do and using their creativity and humor to do something only humans can do.

@Peaty & Oaty
Thanks for the tip! I'm on the mailing list but have been too busy this week to try any. 

Tonight once I have located and defeated all the dust monsters in preparation for a family visit, I will sit back down, cobwebs in hair, glass of good red in hand, and enjoy.

